PROMPT_VERSION = "regent-strategic-planner-prompt-v0111-8"

DECK_GUIDANCE = """\
THE REGENT'S DECK. Stars are a second resource beside energy: some cards gain them, others cost them on top of their energy cost (a card's star_cost), and the starting relic gives a few at the start of each combat. Keep gain and cost in balance. The deck_balance track "stars" compares the Stars the deck's cards gain in one pass through the deck with the Star costs it holds. What the balance should be is known. In 475 solo A10 runs on this game version by the highest-rated Spire Codex players (344 won), the deck's Star costs ran ahead of its Star gain by about one Star at the act 1 boss, two at the act 2 boss and three or four at the act 3 boss, which is what the relic's Stars and the Powers that gain Stars every turn pay for. Those players took a card that costs more Stars than it gains 26 to 30% of the times it was offered while the deck's gain covered its costs, a big one (three Stars or more) four times in ten, and 16 to 19% once the costs were ahead; a card that gains Stars 26 to 31% of the time, and 33 to 39% once three or more Stars were owed. So while the track is short (more than three Stars owed), a Star-cost card will often sit in hand unplayable: take Star gain first and skip further Star costs however strong the card reads. While it is over-supplied (more than three Stars spare), Stars go unspent: a strong Star-cost card or a payoff for gaining and spending Stars is then worth more than its text alone, and plain Star gain is a skip. In between, price a Star-cost card by both costs and check that the deck can pay its Stars in the turn it is drawn.

The deck those players carried into the act 1, 2 and 3 bosses held about 19, 25 and 33 cards and is not kept thin: they took 85% of the card rewards in act 1, 66% in act 2 and 56% in act 3, as often with a large deck as with a small one. It holds few plain attacks and many cards that do something else: two, four and five damage cards against three, six and nine cards with no damage or Block role (Star utility, draw, energy, Powers). A damage card was taken 23% of the time with none added, 11% with two and 7% with four, so deck_balance marks damage over-supplied early (from the fourth in act 1, the fifth in act 2, the sixth after). A Block card was taken four times in ten with none or one added and a quarter of the time after, and Block is never marked over-supplied. Block that grows or repeats (Plating, a Power that gains Block when Stars are spent or when the Sovereign Blade is played) was taken four times in ten at any count and half the time in act 3: deck_balance marks it short below four sources, and it leads after the first picks. Star gain that comes on a Block card serves both needs. The starter holds one card that applies Weak; a second was taken three times in ten and a fourth one in ten (the track "weak" marks a fourth as too many). Draw, energy and Star utility were taken a third of the time in every act whatever the deck held, the best of them two times in three or more: keep adding them. Removals: about three a run, more than half of them in act 2, starter Strikes first.

Scaling comes from one of two routes; count the pieces held and build the one that is ahead instead of splitting. Stars: Powers that pay out every time Stars are gained or spent, or that gain Stars every turn, need a steady flow in both directions, so they follow a balanced "stars" track, not the other way round. The sword: the first Forge of a combat adds Sovereign Blade to the hand and every Forge adds to its damage for that fight, so the route needs several Forge sources that are played early, and then the cards that make the Blade hit more often, hit ALL enemies or return to hand. Those players took a Forge card or another damage-scaling card a bit under a quarter of the time at any count (the winning decks held one or two at the act 1 boss, two or three at the act 2 boss and four at the act 3 boss), and a card that names the Sovereign Blade one time in six with none in the deck but half the time with one or two: the sword route is built on once it exists, not opened late. One Forge card alone is not scaling; several are, and deck_balance counts each as damage scaling.
"""

